+      <article id="journal-text" class="journal-article">
+        <p>
+          At The Daily Grind, exceptional coffee begins long before the first crack in our roasting drum. Every harvest season, our sourcing team travels to high-altitude coffee-growing regions across Ethiopia, Colombia, and Guatemala to work directly with smallholder farmers and family-owned cooperatives. By bypassing traditional commodity middlemen and committing to Direct Trade partnerships, we pay growers at least 35% above Fair Trade minimums—ensuring that the communities tending these trees can invest in regenerative agriculture, clean water infrastructure, and long-term crop resilience.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          We specifically seek out shade-grown Arabica varietals—such as Heirloom, Bourbon, and Caturra—cultivated at elevations between 1,500 and 2,100 meters above sea level. Cooler mountain nights slow the maturation of the coffee cherry, allowing complex sugars and vibrant organic acids to develop inside the seed. During harvest, cherries are hand-picked at peak ripeness rather than strip-harvested, ensuring only dense, ruby-red fruit enters the washing stations and drying patios.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Once harvested, each micro-lot undergoes meticulous post-harvest processing tailored to its terroir. Our Yirgacheffe lots are sun-dried on raised African beds for up to three weeks to preserve bright jasmine and bergamot aromatics, while our Colombian Huila beans undergo a fully washed fermentation in mountain spring water for a crisp, balanced acidity and caramel sweetness. Before any green coffee is cleared for shipment in hermetically sealed GrainPro bags, our certified Q-Graders cup and score multiple sample roasts, selecting only specialty-grade lots that score 86 points or higher.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          When the green beans arrive at our local roastery, we log every bag's moisture content, bean density, and water activity so we can craft a custom roast profile for each harvest. By roasting in small batches and tracking rate-of-rise temperatures in real time, we highlight the natural origin characteristics that farmers spent months cultivating—delivering a transparent, traceable, and deeply flavorful cup of coffee every single morning.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+    </section>
